export const PITCHEI_087 = {
  '1#_': `For prohibition of eating. And even not in the manner of eating and benefit it is forbidden and one would receive lashes for it, unlike other prohibitions except kilaei hakerem — so Rambam chapter 14 Laws of Forbidden Foods; Pri Megadim brought this in the introduction.`,
  '2#_': `For prohibition of benefit. And if one would receive lashes for benefit — Rambam's view is that one does not receive lashes, and other poskim hold that one does receive lashes; the practical difference today is whether one receives lashes. Beit HaTaharah above siman 16 s.k. 10. And see Pri Megadim in the introduction that he was uncertain whether chelev shemen is forbidden in cooking and benefit d'oraisa — for in eating we hold chelev shemen is forbidden d'oraisa, and for cooking and benefit it is uncertain — see there. And see Tzalach in Pesachim daf 22 s.v. and behold a nursing mother — he was also uncertain regarding this for benefit alone, and afterward wrote plainly that according to Rambam one certainly does not receive lashes for benefit, and therefore regarding prohibition, chelev shemen is also forbidden for benefit d'oraisa; but for other poskim who receive lashes for benefit, one certainly does not receive lashes for benefit except for a kezayit — see there. And see in responsum of Rabbeinu Akiva Eiger siman 195 that he brought proof from the sugya in Bava Kamma daf 41 that its measure is in benefit of a perutah — see there. And basar b'chalav that becomes forbidden for benefit — one buries it. And if one burned it, its ash is also forbidden for benefit d'oraisa. And regarding whether it is permitted to feed it to dogs — see below siman 94 s.k. 5.`,
  '3#_': `By way of cooking. Beit HaTaharah — and see Peri Chadash that frying is also included in cooking; but Magen Avraham in Seder Limud general rule 5 and Maharam Schiff at end of the book on Chullin inclined to be lenient that frying is not included in cooking and is not forbidden for benefit. And in Peri Megadim one may be lenient. And hot in hot without gravy — Pri Megadim wrote in the introduction in name of Rashal that it is not by way of cooking and is permitted for benefit — see there. [And see in responsum Chasam Sofer siman 97 what he wrote in our time; nevertheless even in these that are only d'rabbanan and permitted for benefit one must give them to a non-Jew thus and not cook initially, for then one violates cooking and it is forbidden for benefit d'oraisa, for although the piece absorbed not by way of cooking, nevertheless afterward the meat cooked with the milk absorbed in it — this is obvious; and so in responsum Noda Biyehudah Yoreh Deah siman 26 s.v. and from this.]`,
  '4#_': `For benefit. Beit HaTaharah — and see in responsum Shaar Efraim siman 38 that he ruled regarding butter that was cooked in a pot of meat in a kli rishon — forbidden to light with it to illuminate the house because it is forbidden for benefit; and also to light Chanukah lights — although mitzvot are not for benefit, nevertheless forbidden since for Chanukah one needs a measure, and in basar b'chalav since forbidden for benefit the measure is broken — see there (see Pri Megadim that he copied in his name that for Chanukah it is permitted, and Maharam Chagiz did not examine there well; and so in responsum Orach Chaim siman 573 in his name that for Chanukah too it is forbidden). And see in responsum Shamayim Rosh siman 285 in Hagahot Kesef Nivchar there what he wrote on words of Shaar Efraim; and see in Or Zaru'a in Orach Chaim there that he wondered — behold in any case it is forbidden because of cooking basar b'chalav. And see in responsum Shvut Yaakov part 1 siman 38 and in his responsum there what he wrote; and see in responsum Rabbi Akiva Eiger in omissions to siman 84.`,
  '5#_': `In meat. See Maharam Schiff at end of Chullin that he wrote regarding liver that they wanted to fry it in fat and fried it in butter and inclined to be lenient — liver is blood and "kid" the Torah forbade, not blood (he also inclined there that frying is not cooking). And see Pri Megadim that wrote that for this alone one certainly should not rely and be lenient based on "kid" and not blood; and Maharam Schiff did not write this except as a mere side point. And see in responsum Shamayim Rosh siman 285 in Hagahot Kesef Nivchar there what he wrote on words of Maharam Schiff.`,
  '6#_': `Impure. See in Derech Mitzvosecha that he wrote: nevelah meat and likewise milk — forbidden to cook in milk d'oraisa and one receives lashes for cooking it; but for eating there is no basar b'chalav prohibition — all this is explained in Rambam chapter 9 Laws of Forbidden Foods law 6. However whether it is forbidden for benefit is not explained there, but in commentary on Mishnayot chapter 3 of Keritut it is explained that it is not forbidden for benefit; and although according to Tosafot in Chullin 101 s.v. inclusive prohibition that they wrote that prohibition of benefit is not called an additional prohibition — there is no necessity for Rambam's words there; nevertheless we do not find that Tosafot in their explanation disagree with Rambam's ruling that where there is no eating prohibition because of basar b'chalav, there is no benefit prohibition; therefore one who relies on this in a case of loss should not lose — end. And see Pri Megadim in the introduction that wrote that we rule it is forbidden for benefit d'oraisa — see there. (And see in responsum Chasam Sofer siman 92 that they also asked on this matter and brought words of the Gaon Me'il Tzedakah in Kanfei Yonah siman 87 regarding an incident where craftsmen mixed butter into milk to make candles because milk was expensive and he inclined to forbid it for benefit; although from the reason of tarbus one may say that candles of milk are not made only from kidney and abdominal milk that they mix many types of milk and membranes that have no karet — if so there is basar b'chalav because of mixture of these milks; but nevertheless because of nevelah there is concern, for most animals of non-Jews are not slaughtered, and nevertheless the Gaon forbade for benefit — if so he holds that although there is no eating prohibition, nevertheless there is benefit prohibition. He expanded on this and concludes that certainly the rabbi who ruled is like the Gaon Kanfei Yonah; and so it appears from many great Acharonim. And even for Derech Mitzvosecha he does not deal with benefit of lighting; but a kreuzel candle to light by hand — there is concern for cooking basar b'chalav because the drop around the flame cooks; and although for Shabbat this is not considered cooking, for basar b'chalav it may be cooking. He also wrote: however a kreuzel candle that became forbidden as above and was mixed with others — plainly nullified, for this is not davar sheb'minyan, only specifically Chanukah lights where each night they count the number of lights for its mitzvah — see there. And see in responsum Shvut Yaakov part 1 siman 60.)`,
  '7#_': `And meat of wild animal and fowl, etc. And likewise the reverse — meat of pure domestic animal in milk of pure wild animal is not forbidden d'oraisa. Tiferet Lemoshe — see there.`,
  '8#_': `Permitted in cooking. See in Chumdei Daniel 18 that he wrote that it is permitted to cook meat for a non-Jew in a pot of milk — it is not a kli rishon, for whatever is forbidden only d'rabbanan they did not decree on cooking and benefit; and here there is no marit ayin — who would know whether it is a pot of meat or milk? Nevertheless one should be stringent because Rama wrote in seif 6 that it is forbidden to stoke, etc. And one may say because initially it was pure milk they were somewhat stringent — see there.`,
  '9#_': `Prohibition. Beit HaTaharah — and see in responsum Chinuch Beit Yosef siman 61 that he supports words of Levush that it is forbidden to eat fish in milk because he investigated with expert physicians that specifically fish fried with butter is permitted to eat, but fish cooked in milk there is danger of illness — fish cool very much and milk is bad and harmful to the body — see there [and see in responsum Chasam Sofer siman 101 that wrote this is not true and we rely on Rambam, the great of physicians]. And so Pri Megadim in name of BeHag that fish with milk is dangerous, and in butter and fat absorbed on the milk there is no danger — see there, that it appears his view is that even with milk specifically l'chatchila one should be careful, but b'dieved one should not forbid. And see in responsum Adnei Paz siman 42 that wrote in name of Rabbeinu Bachya in parashat Mishpatim that there is grave danger in fish with cheese, and the same with milk — therefore he ruled that even b'dieved it is forbidden; but fish with butter he wrote is permitted since everyone does so — see there. And it appears to me that nowadays since everyone cooks also with milk it is permitted, for since the many have trodden upon it, etc.`,
  '10#_': `Almond milk. Beit HaTaharah — what Maharshal ruled, etc. And see in Nitzotz that I wrote that nevertheless one should not forbid placing fowl meat in almond milk without placing almonds beside it, except if before viewers as at great feasts where there is full marit ayin; but in one's house it is permitted — see there. And what Beit HaTaharah and Taz wrote, etc. — one should not forbid eating, etc. — Taz implies from his words that the same law applies to domestic animal meat.`,
  '11#_': `But if it does not have. Eshel Avraham s.k. 16 — what he challenged on Beit Yosef's explanation of entangled in sinews; and see in Shaar HaMelekh chapter 9 Laws of Forbidden Foods what he wrote to establish Mechaber's view — see there.`,
};

export const RAE_087 = {
  '1#_': `(siman 87 seif א') אלא by way of cooking. ע' בPeri Chadash דגם צלי וpouring וcold לתוך חם הוי d'oraisa וע' ביש"ש chapter 3 (סי' מ"ה) דwrote דmeat חם שנגע לcheese חמה there is למימר דforbidden רק מd'rabbanan עיי"ש וwrote בhagahah of ש"ד שat the end הספר (general rule 40 סי' ד') דחתיכה שנכבשה בחלב forbidden לבשלה דעד כאן הוי איסור pickled מd'rabbanan ועתה עובר על איסור cooking d'oraisa end of his words it appears דאם was cookedה בחלב permitted לחזור ולבשלה וע' בPeri Megadim במשבצות זהב (siman 105 sixty-two):`,
  '2#_': `(Shach s.k. ב) and so too הMordechai פ' אין rennetין. עיי"ש שהביא proof מהא דאמרינן מפני מה אסרו cheeses non-Jew מפני שrennetים אותם בskin קיבת nevelah ואמאי אינו forbidden ג"כ בbenefit because בBach אuntil here כיון דהוי רק by way of כבישה d'rabbanan אינו forbidden בbenefit וקשה לי הא באמת קשה למאי נקט the taste דrennetים בskin קיבת נבלה הא גם בskin קיבת כשירה הוי בBach וכן הקשו Tosafot ותירצו כיון דהוי רק בBach d'rabbanan לא we are concerned לספיקא וכן למה שתירצו הR' Yitzchak מינ"ש והRambam דבאמת יש מהstam sixty בחלב ואיסורו רק rennet וע"י rennet לא נעשה בBach וautomatically בזה אזדא ג"כ ראייתם דregarding benefit because בBach there is not דהא because rennet לא נעשה בBach וכיון דהMordechai לא he holds להנך תירוצים ישאר קושית Tosafot דלמאי נקט nevelah הא בכשירה ג"כ forbidden because בBach וrequires study גם קשה לי על תירוץ הMordechai דcheeses לא נאסרו בbenefit דpickled permitted בbenefit וכן לפwhat he wrote דלTosafot regarding benefit הוי doubt d'rabbanan nevertheless עדיין קשה יהא cheeses דnon-Jew forbiddenים לעשות מהם dish דאם הnon-Jew העמיד בskin ונabsorbed טעם basar b'chalav בכבישה כשעתה cooking אותו הabsorbed בתוך the milk הוי עתה cooking וhe transgressed because cooking בBach וגם בבשלו יהא forbidden בbenefit וrequires study:`,
  '3#_': `(בhagahah) permitted בbenefit. והא דלא wrote דpermitted בcooking that is דכייל בBach ע"י כבישה וsaltingה בזה forbidden בcooking כיון דכבישה וsaltingה נabsorbed בו the milk ובשיבשל הוא cooking עתה הmeat עם the milk Shabbatetc. ועובר because cooking וגם יאסר בbenefit דנעשה בBach d'oraisa investigate ולגרום כן לערב basar b'chalav להנות ממנו ע' במנ"י כלל פ"ה s.k. ט:`,
  '4#_': `(Taz s.k. ב) ובדרישה etc.. ועתה עובר על איסור cooking ש"ת:`,
  '5#_': `(Shach seif קטן ו') שוב בא לידי ספר Be'er Sheva. ועיין בספרו צידה לby way of פ' צו:`,
  '6#_': `(Taz s.k. ד') כמו בחלב אשה. זה תמוה דהא בחלב אשה even דיעבד forbidden ואינו permitted רק בfell לתוך dish דאינו ניכר גם לפי proof זו יהיה proven דגם בdomestic animal meat אין אוסרים אכילתו וכן מה שwrote הTaz וכן it appears language of הרמ"א שwrote יש להניח etc. הא זה deals with בdomestic animal meat:`,
  '7#_': `(seif ד') ואם fell לתוך the dish. ואם עירב במזיד in a dish forbidden לו ולמי שנעשה כן בשבילו ע' above (סי' ס"ו בShach סקי"א):`,
  '8#_': `(שם בhagahah) או meat טמא. קשה לי דלתסר ג"כ impure meat בimpure milk because marit ayin:`,
  '9#_': `(Shach s.k. ו) אבל because מ"ע לא deals with. וכן I found להדיא בתשו' תולדות אblood להRashba (סי' ר'):`,
  '10#_': `(בא"ד) שיבשל לצורך medicinal use. קשה לי דnevertheless אמאי permitted בbenefit ניחוש לmarit ayin וrequires study:`,
  '11#_': `(seif ו') one who cooks meat בmilk-water. קשה לי דwhat he wrote meat wild animal וfowl דשרי בcooking ובbenefit כיון דהוא d'rabbanan ה"נ milk-water וראיתי בתפארת למשה שwrote לdisagreed דהני דהכא שהם מן מיני domestic animal טהורה שנאסר מd'oraisa וnevertheless קשה לי היכן מצינו כן דמההיא דגוזל דfellה לכמכא וכן fish שcame up בbowl של meat דpermitted to eat them בkutach because noten taam bar noten taam even דkutach הוא בmilk-water וזהו ראיית Tosafot מזה proven רק דבאכילה forbidden אבל cooking לא proven ואם מלישנא דone who cooks בmilk-water exempt דit appears exempt אבל forbidden וas written הRosh הא י"ל כמו דמפרשי' כל הmeat forbidden לבשל namely לאכול ה"נ י"ל דone who cooks בmilk-water that is האוכל [ואדרבה בזה מרווח סוגיא יותר במה דפרכינן מהך baraita על מ"ד דone who cooks חלב בחלב אינו לוקה דהא בהך baraita פיגול וleftover וטמא שבשלו בחלב liable דקשה דלמא הbaraita deals with רק regarding cooking וכן challenged הMaharsha ותירוצו דפירכת הGemara ללישנא דכיון דאאכילה לא לקי אcooking נמי לא לקי דחוק קצת גם language of Rashi אינו teacher כן שwrote אלמא דkid רביי' והו"ל לומר אלמא דעכ"פ אcooking היה לקי ולפי הit appears to me דכיון דקתני בהך baraita one who cooks בmilk-water exempt דuntil here דdeals with מאכילה דcooking even l'chatchila permitted ה"נ סיפא leftover ופיגול deals with מאכילה investigate] וrequires study וביותר קשה לי על blood בחלב דלמאי אמרו חand these are his words לthey decreed באיסור בBach הא without"ה לא because blood וif so הוי כמו טמאה דpermitted cooking וbenefit לכ"ע וצע"ג:`,
};

export const SIFTEI_087 = {
  '1#א': `["That it is not forbidden from the Torah except by way of cooking, etc. Meaning: but not by pickling or salting, which is not a way of cooking."]`,
  '1#ב': `["All basar b'chalav, etc. — permitted for benefit. Tur and Orach Chaim general rule 40 s.v. and so Mechaber's view in siman 63 that fowl meat in milk, which is forbidden in eating only d'rabbanan, is permitted for benefit even d'rabbanan; and so Raavad in Sefer HaChinuch mitzvah 113; and so Rambam chapter 9 of Forbidden Foods law 4 explicitly; and so he explained in his commentary on the Mishnah chapter 25; and so the Rav the Maggid explained his view, and so Maharshal understood chapter 25 siman 100 and other Acharonim — Rambam's view, not as Bach wrote that Rambam holds fowl meat in milk is forbidden for benefit d'rabbanan as it is forbidden in eating d'rabbanan (and what he wrote, strained to say this 'You shall not cook' is for eating, etc. — I already answered in my book see there); and so Mordechai chapter Ein Maamidin and Hagahot Ashiri last chapter of Avodah Zarah from Mahari Weil that by pickling or salting it is permitted for benefit; and so Mechaber below siman 91 — and it is from Agur that Beit Yosef brought there and Maharshal there part of Avodah Zarah — and his words are not required."]`,
  '3#א': `["But pure meat in impure milk, etc. Tur's language: but pure meat in milk of an impure animal, or impure meat in pure milk, and meat of wild animal and fowl — even in pure milk — is only d'rabbanan and permitted in cooking and benefit — end. Beit Yosef wrote: the wording is imprecise when he says 'only d'rabbanan' — it can refer only to wild animal and fowl in pure milk; for pure meat in impure milk or the reverse we do not find the Sages forbade eating because of basar b'chalav, and there was no reason to forbid it rabbinically after it is already forbidden and standing from the Torah because of impure meat or impure milk, etc. — end. Therefore he corrected the wording here and aimed well; but Bach challenged him and explained Tur comes to teach us that nevertheless rabbinically it is forbidden because of basar b'chalav, and there is a difference regarding a piece becoming nevelah, for he holds (below siman 92) only with meat and milk; and likewise regarding a piece fit to honor guests — see there; and so Perishah explained. Therefore even for us, who hold noten taam noten taam in all prohibitions, there would have been practical difference regarding what the Rav wrote in hagahah siman 92 seif 4 — see there and investigate. In my humble opinion this is wrong: we certainly do not find anywhere, nor did any posek omit writing this; and from Rashi and Ran (chapter 25 daf 113a in the Mishnah) it appears explicitly there is no prohibition because of basar b'chalav at all, even rabbinically; and there is no reason the Sages would forbid it because of basar b'chalav — for with fowl meat a decree fits well: if they ate fowl in milk they would also eat domestic animal meat in milk, as Rambam wrote there; but what relevance to decree that pure domestic meat in impure milk or the reverse be forbidden because of basar b'chalav, for without this there is nothing to fear since it is already forbidden because of impure meat or impure milk — and this appears Beit Yosef's intent in what he wrote 'and there was no reason to forbid it,' not as Bach's wording that it is already forbidden in eating from the Torah and they need not decree — and he understood Beit Yosef comes to say there is no difference whether forbidden because of basar b'chalav since already forbidden from the Torah, and therefore he answered there is a difference, etc.; rather Beit Yosef's intent is as I explained, and so is primary for law, since a piece fit to honor guests itself is rabbinic as below siman 110; also Derishah wrote at the end of his words: but it is still difficult that we do not find anywhere that pure meat in impure milk or the reverse has a rabbinic prohibition and becomes nevelah rabbinically — perforce."]`,
  '3#ב': `["And meat of wild animal and fowl, etc. — and even in eating, etc. Mechaber's view: meat of wild animal and fowl is not forbidden in eating even by cooking, only rabbinically, and permitted for benefit and in cooking entirely; Maharshal and Bach relied on Mahari Weil in hagahah ShaDa siman 76 where he wrote it is difficult to be lenient against Tosafot — therefore they ruled wild animal and fowl in milk is d'oraisa; in my book I upheld those who say it is rabbinic from the Gemara, and I brought there that so is the view of many poskim, and even Mahari Weil only said it is difficult to be lenient against Tosafot not to eat cheese immediately after fowl meat, but he admits the law is fowl in milk is rabbinic, for he wrote in hagahah ShaDa siman 69 complete eggs are permitted in milk because fowl in milk is rabbinic and they were lenient (and so R' Nissim in what he wrote siman 69 that Mahari Weil holds primary like Beit HaGefen — see there); and although Tosafot tied the law of eating cheese after fowl to whether fowl in milk is d'oraisa or rabbinic, nevertheless it is not required as written there; and from Tosafot themselves there it appears it is not required to say so, only they came to reconcile world practice — see there. In truth one may say another way: even as we hold fowl in milk is rabbinic, it is forbidden to eat cheese immediately after it, as below siman 89; and the baraita 'wage of fowl and cheese are eaten at dessert' we explain as Rambam and his supporters explain, and Beit Yosef brings siman 89 — meaning one eats cheese and afterward fowl; and there is further to distinguish in several aspects; and Rambam wrote end of chapter 2 of Laws of Rebels that one who says fowl in milk is forbidden from the Torah violates bal tosif — see there."]`,
  '3#ג': `["But fish, etc. Taz wrote: nevertheless one should not eat fish in milk because of danger, as explained in Orach Chaim siman 173 — end. In truth he followed Beit Yosef who wrote so, but it is an error, for there only fish with meat is forbidden because of danger, but with milk we have not heard nor seen; every day we practice cooking fish in milk and eating; Darkei Moshe also challenged Beit Yosef, and in Be'er Sheva daf 100 and other Acharonim challenged him; and in the Gemara chapter Kol HaTzlamim (daf 76b) it appears explicitly there is not even because of danger — see there — and it is simple."]`,
  '3#ד': `["They practice making almond milk, etc. since, etc. Meaning: and one could say regarding fish blood siman 66 seif 6 that it is entirely permitted; nevertheless if placed in a vessel it is forbidden because of marit ayin unless there are scales recognizable as fish blood — for there the blood itself is d'oraisa forbidden, therefore the viewer will say he eats animal blood forbidden d'oraisa; but fowl meat in milk itself is rabbinic and there is no concern for marit ayin; therefore domestic animal meat in milk, which is d'oraisa forbidden, one should place almonds near it so the viewer not think it is real milk — and so in the sage there end of general rule 62; therefore it must speak of cooking, for otherwise even domestic animal meat has no d'oraisa prohibition and need not place almonds — and so is clear in the sage there explicitly — see there and in signs of the sage. But Maharshal chapter 25 siman 52 learned from fish blood on Purim and similar when they regularly eat chicken in almond milk — forbidden unless he places almonds inside and beside for recognition, especially with fowl meat where there is more concern lest they say fowl in milk is permitted even rabbinically like R' Yosi HaGelili — end; and Acharonim agreed, and so it appears to me (again Be'er Sheva came to hand and I found he ruled so): for even in rabbinic matters we are concerned for marit ayin in many places, including Gemara chapter Bamah Beheimah (Shabbat daf 54) 'and not a pair' even though plugged — lest it appear one goes to the market — Rashi: to the market to sell, and this is marit ayin, as in chapter Bamah Ishah (end Shabbat daf 64) and agreed by all poskim, as explained in Orach Chaim siman 305 although commerce on Shabbat is only rabbinic, as in Orach Chaim and Choshen Mishpat siman 235; and so is clear in Tosafot Ketubot chapter Afelu (daf 60a) s.v. mima'akan, etc. — that even in rabbinic matters we are concerned for marit ayin — and so appears in many places."]`,
  '4#א': `["And it appears to me therefore all the more, etc. Per Raavad's view it appears one must resolve that Taz wrote in siman 63 they are permitted in cooking and benefit — it is the arranged Mishnah in chapter 25 and agreed by all poskim — meaning permitted by law, but not because of marit ayin; and there is difference in cases where there is no concern for marit ayin such as for medical need, etc., as Maharshal wrote chapter 25 siman 102 regarding woman's milk that for medical need it is permitted even without any danger since there is no prohibition at all, only marit ayin — end of his words. Or you find it in other cases where there is no marit ayin concern, and thereby what the Rav left in the sage — Rashba's words in wonder — is resolved. Indeed poskim who wrote plainly 'permitted in cooking' imply even for marit ayin there is none — and so appear Maharshal's words chapter 25 and so appears Mechaber's view since he did not write impure animal milk itself is forbidden because of marit ayin; therefore it appears clear to me it is different there since in any case one of them is forbidden in eating — certainly in cooking alone there is no marit ayin, for one may cook for medical need or other purposes; and 'forbidden to cook them' means to eat them, as Mechaber wrote siman 95 'forbidden to cook them' — therefore must also explain 'to eat them'; and we find this in Scripture and Mishnah — they derived eating in the language of cooking — see there; and only in such a case is there marit ayin concern; and therefore per the Rav too one must say so, for even with domestic animal meat in almond milk he holds one need not place almonds except in cooking — as in the previous s.k.; and if so how did they cook together at first — great strain to say during cooking he placed almonds by the pot; rather certainly in cooking alone there is no marit ayin, therefore 'forbidden to cook meat in woman's milk' means to eat; and therefore impure animal meat in pure milk or the reverse — since they do not cook to eat, for in any case forbidden because of impure meat or milk — there is no marit ayin as above; and thereby Rashba's words the Rav left in the sage in wonder are resolved well; or with impure meat there is no marit ayin concern since recognizable as impure meat, and so impure milk as in Gemara Avodah Zarah (daf 35b) — pure milk is white, impure green; but woman's milk and almond milk are not recognizable and forbidden because of marit ayin — and thereby Rashba's words are resolved well; nevertheless the first explanation is primary."]`,
  '4#ב': `["But with fowl, which is rabbinic, there is no concern. This follows his view in seif 3; but per what he wrote in s.k. 6, with fowl too there is concern — and Rashba and Mechaber stated plainly without distinguishing domestic animal from fowl."]`,
  '5#א': `["Even though entangled in sinews, etc. And what appears above siman 86 seif 4 — when he struck the chicken on its tail and it laid eggs forbidden because of eiver min ha'chai if entangled in sinews — although completed in white and yolk, since entangled it is considered chicken meat; this case is different regarding basar b'chalav of fowl which is rabbinic and they were lenient; or this case is different since each alone is permitted — so Acharonim wrote; and this distinction is mentioned in Tosafot and poskim and Rashba from their words to distinguish between nevelah egg above siman 86 seif 3 which is forbidden and here; and it appears to me without this there is no difficulty from striking the chicken on its tail — for although not considered meat, even actual milk would be forbidden because of eiver min ha'chai if not that the verse revealed 'a land flowing with milk and honey' — as above siman 81 s.k. 11; and if so although the verse revealed milk is permitted because not attached to anything and not separating from meat — but eggs entangled and attached certainly remain in their prohibition; greater still some forbid even milk-water because of eiver min ha'chai — as above siman 81; and although we do not hold like them for those reasons explained there — nevertheless certainly entangled eggs are forbidden by all because of eiver min ha'chai; and Rabbenu Tam also wrote even actual eggs would be forbidden because of eiver min ha'chai if not that the verse permitted — as Sha'arim wrote siman 64 and other poskim in his name; and therefore certainly when entangled, since the verse did not reveal, they are forbidden because of eiver min ha'chai — and this is clear."]`,
  '5#ב': `["But if it has only yolk, etc. It appears even if not entangled — all that did not complete except the yolk is like meat itself, as Rashba wrote; in my book I expanded on this and brought that l'chatchila one should not eat in milk even those completed in yolk and white while the shell is still soft even if not entangled — per Rashbam, Mordechai, Agudah first chapter of Beitzah, Sha'arim siman 76, Orach Chaim, and Mahari Weil in hagahah ShaDa; but b'dieved if cooked with milk it appears permitted; but if entangled, even b'dieved forbidden, since Beit HaGefen daf 35 side 3 ruled like R' Yaakov, and so in all Rif manuscripts before us in first chapter of Beitzah, and Sheltei HaGibborim chapter 25 daf 295a in name of Rif; and Ran's view is clear if not misread in chapter 25; and so R' Yonatan brought in Orach Chaim and Kol Bo siman 106; and Rashbam and his supporters clearly hold halachah is like R' Yaakov — otherwise cannot resolve the sugya in first chapter of Beitzah per their words; and Agudah part 81 explicitly; and Yerushalmi ruled explicitly like R' Yaakov; and our Gemara somewhat implies they return to establish the baraita like him, unlike Semag there who left a difficulty that Rif ruled like Ra'avyah; also Beit Yosef and Acharonim wrote plainly all poskim ruled like first tanna — and so I wrote; and I proved 'complete' means completed in yolk and white — if soft shell entangled, even b'dieved forbidden; if not entangled, permitted; but small ones that completed only in yolk, even not entangled, forbidden even b'dieved as like meat itself — and so in the sage general rule 70; except he did not distinguish entangled or not, and from his words there those completed in yolk and white, even entangled, are permitted b'dieved — primary as I wrote; also requires study why he did not emend here; nevertheless it appears even if only yolk completed and entangled, one may be lenient where there is great loss — and such is worthy to rely on Rashi, Tosafot, Rosh, Tur, and Rabbenu Yerucham in such a case; also it appears per them 'entangled' means small ones completed only in yolk entangled in spine meat — not as Beit Yosef wrote per Tur in name of Rashi when separated from spine meat with red streaks; afterward Taz wrote Rashi himself did not explain so in either version; both Gemara versions clearly contradict Beit Yosef — rather as I explained; Maharshal's words chapter 25 siman 52 are wondrous on this and contradict each other and what he wrote first chapter of Beitzah siman 19 — see there; all this is law; but Mahari Weil wrote women do not practice leniency even when fully complete and outer shell fully hard — see there; Maharshal and the Rav in the sage followed this custom; however from Mahari Weil's and their words it is mere stringency; therefore where there is no custom, no concern at all; Bach sought to find the custom is law — his words are not required — see there."]`,
  '5#ג': `["Forbidden to cook them in milk. This wording Taz also wrote; he is uncertain — for to cook alone is obviously permitted, for even fowl itself was explained above siman 63 permitted in cooking even rabbinically; rather 'to cook them' means to eat them. Law of salting eggs found in fowl was explained above siman 87 — see there."]`,
  '5#ד': `["But if he ate them by themselves. Even if entangled still in the cluster — so in Orach Chaim there; and it appears there even if only yolk completed and entangled in cluster, permitted to eat cheese or milk afterward — see there; and 'entangled in cluster' Rashi explained: attached in spine meat."]`,
  '6#א': `["One is not flogged for it. But there is nevertheless a prohibition; and so milk of a dead animal — there is a prohibition, as below siman 90 regarding udder, and as the Rav wrote in hagahah nearby; and all imply there is a prohibition even in male milk; and what the Rav wrote in hagahah — male milk is not called milk at all, etc. — will be explained in s.k. 16."]`,
  '6#ב': `["In milk of a dead animal. Meaning milk that came out after its death; and 'dead' means by itself or slaughtered, as below siman 90 regarding udder — investigate."]`,
  '6#ג': `["And one is not flogged for eating it because of basar b'chalav. This wording Rambam wrote; it appears he holds it is because of basar b'chalav that one is not flogged for eating it — whereas for blood one is flogged for eating even if cooked, for he holds one who cooks blood violates it; however we hold blood cooked or salted is only rabbinic, as above siman 69 s.k. 39 and in many places."]`,
  '6#ד': `["And male milk is not called milk, etc. It appears since it is not called milk there is not even a rabbinic prohibition forbidding the food; Orach Chaim there wrote it is no worse than woman's milk; from the Rav the Maggid chapter 9 Forbidden Foods it appears male milk is forbidden rabbinically, and he wrote so appears from Rambam whom Mechaber copied; and somewhat in Gemara chapter 25 (daf 113b) Shmuel said there: in its mother's milk and not male milk, in its mother's milk and not in milk of slaughtered animal; if so since one verse excludes them and milk of slaughtered animal we hold is rabbinically forbidden as below siman 90 — so too male milk; and the Maggid appears to discuss male milk of animal, as Rashi explained there: and not male milk that had little milk from teats such as if it changed and grew teats; so Maharshal there siman 100; and Gemara clearly implies: afterward he asked one who cooks milk of a kid that did not nurse — what is the law? He said: since Shmuel needed to say in its mother's milk and not male milk — male milk does not come under 'if'; but this one since it comes under 'if' is forbidden — learn male milk of animal; but male milk of man is obviously no worse than woman's milk; therefore Mechaber who copied Rambam implies also male milk is exempt but forbidden; and the Rav in hagahah did not disagree."]`,
  '6#ה': `["And milk-water, etc. And it is written in Orach Chaim there that even that milk-water remaining after they make cheese forbids the food — end."]`,
  '6#ו': `["And some say it is forbidden to stoke the fire, etc. And Mordechai there wrote it is forbidden to cook forbidden meat in an Israelite's house in a gentile's pot lest the Israelite stoke it — therefore one needs two gentile maidservants, one pot of meat and one of milk; however this is only a mere stringency and people are not careful about it — end."]`,
  '6#ז': `["Forbidden for benefit. Meaning: when he rinsed them in boiling water and mixed them so boiling — if not, it is not forbidden for benefit since it is not forbidden from the Torah except by way of cooking, as above seif 1 and seif 3 — end."]`,
  '6#ח': `["And habit to mix meat and milk there. And that it is not forbidden from this for head-washing — for one benefits — since it is not a matter of eating it is not considered benefit when one does not benefit from the body of the prohibition, as will be explained below siman 94 see there; and above siman 108 seif 4 in hagahah and siman 155 in hagahah — end."]`,
  '6#ט': `["And even l'chatchila, etc. See in the sage general rule 76; and it appears there that in metal pots one need not be stringent even l'chatchila, for even if something splashed it would return and become white by the fire — see there — end."]`,
  '7#_': `Exempt. And so one who eats them, etc. — likewise there is nevertheless a prohibition.`,
  '8#_': `They are not in the category of milk-water. See above siman 81 seif 5 and what is written there — end.`,
  '9#א': `["It is not milk. For it derives from the category of food and is considered excrement and filth — end."]`,
  '9#ב': `["And some forbid. Meaning: when clear, as written above siman 81 seif 6; and on this the Rav wrote in hagahah and so they practice; and he distinguished in hagahah in seif 10 between dried and clear; and it appears from the sage general rule 65 that l'chatchila it is permitted when dried — see there; however regarding clear his view is that even b'dieved it is forbidden, as he wrote in hagahah in seif 11; nevertheless it appears l'chatchila it is forbidden even when dried because of marit ayin, as written in seif 4; and see s.k. 7 and Maharshal chapter 25 siman 107 who ruled even dried is considered actual milk by law, as Rashi; and so appears Ran's view (and so Rokeach siman 461, and requires study, for in siman 464 it appears he distinguishes dried and clear — see there); and so Orach Chaim beginning of general rule 46 — end."]`,
  '10#א': `["L'chatchila one should not place it, etc. The reason is explained there that stomach milk is sharp and sour since they set rennet in it — end."]`,
  '10#ב': `["That it was salted in its stomach. Beit Yosef wrote: and the measure required for it to remain in salting to be considered boiling was explained in siman 69 regarding salting in a vessel that is not perforated; and the measure of its salting to be boiling Mechaber wrote in siman 91 — end; if so his view is it must remain in salting until they place it on fire and it begins to boil, as above siman 69 seif 18; and it must be salted so much that it is not eaten because of its salt, as below siman 91 seif 5; and it appears that even for us, who hold salting forbids immediately, and even if not salted except a little as they salt for roasting — nevertheless one may permit here b'dieved if they set rennet in it when not salted so much, since in any case many poskim and Mechaber among them consider it mere excrement even when clear — end."]`,
  '10#ג': `["Or it stood in it a day. Meaning: by itself, for it is like pickled — end."]`,
  '10#ד': `["Forbidden to set rennet in it. It appears there is no distinction between dried and clear — and this is l'chatchila; and although one may cook when dried even l'chatchila, as written in s.k. 25 there — that case is different since there is no prohibition except because of milk, and since dried it is like excrement; but here, although excrement, since it was salted or stood by itself in the stomach, it certainly received taste from the stomach skin and therefore l'chatchila forbidden to set rennet in it to make cheese; however b'dieved Mechaber's view in his book Beit Yosef is to permit in every case, and therefore he stated here without distinction; and the Rav in hagahah does not agree — end."]`,
  '10#ה': `["Until there are sixty against the milk, etc. Meaning: until there are sixty against the milk in which rennet was set and cheeses were made, against the milk that was in the stomach forbidden in the stomach because of basar b'chalav; and apparently it is difficult: why does sixty suffice? For the milk in the stomach is forbidden in itself and became nevelah, and we say below siman 101 that the law of a piece fit to honor guests applies only when the prohibition is because of itself such as nevelah and basar b'chalav — perforce; if so here, since it is a setting agent, even in a thousand it is not nullified, as written in seif 11; and so Mordechai chapter 25 and responsum of Maimonides siman 18 see there; responsum of R' Shimon bar Avraham in a case where they made cheese from milk in a stomach and afterward found in the sack a little of the thin milk — the cheeses are forbidden since the prohibition sets — meaning it imparts taste — end; and although Tur also wrote as the Rav wrote, one may say Tur holds a setting agent that is forbidden nullifies in sixty, as Rabbenu Tam in Mordechai and in responsum of Maimonides there; and one may say specifically a setting agent forbidden in itself such as nevelah we say it is not nullified, unlike here where the setting agent is not forbidden in itself but because of mixture of basar b'chalav — therefore it nullifies well (and even if we say actual basar b'chalav forbidden by cooking is considered the substance of the prohibition and if one set rennet afterward it forbids in any amount — nevertheless here, since forbidden only by salting and pickling which is only rabbinic, the Rav holds it is not considered substance of prohibition regarding setting in any amount — see there; however Orach Chaim general rule 47 law 8 asks whether pickling is considered like cooked for this and it appears the Rav is lenient on this); and responsum of R' Shimon bar Avraham follows his view that even setting in kosher stomach skin is not nullified, as explained in his words there; but for us, who do not hold so, as Mechaber wrote in seif 11 — if so, although we forbid the milk because it absorbed from the stomach, nevertheless sixty suffices — investigate, and it is somewhat strained — end."]`,
  '10#ו': `["And if the stomach was dried, etc. Meaning: if the milk in the stomach was dried it forbids nothing even if there were not sixty against the milk in which rennet was set, against the milk in the stomach; the reason: when dried the milk is considered mere excrement and one cannot say it received taste from the stomach skin and imparts taste to the cheeses, and it is not second taste permitting the first, for the second taste of prohibition is like onions absorbed from meat and cooked in a milk pot — Taz siman 94 seif 6 wrote one needs at least sixty against the meat in the onions; and here too we would need sixty against the milk that was in the stomach, for we do not know how much it absorbed from the stomach; but this case is different, for there is no power in taste that this excrement received from the skin to when it later joins actual cheese milk to carry meat taste with it and make basar b'chalav — worse than second taste, since it is pure excrement; and Taz wrote: although it nevertheless received meat taste and again imparts that taste to cheeses set in it, it is only second taste permitting the first, like fish that rose into a bowl permitted to eat them in kutach, as will be explained below siman 95 — it appears to me — end; and he did not aim well, for we do not hold regarding fish in a bowl that it is second taste permitting the first before it came to prohibition, for meat imparted taste to the bowl and the bowl to fish — second taste permitting the first, and therefore permitted to eat them afterward in kutach; but here it is only one second taste permitting the first — the stomach in dried milk; and all poskim agree in such a case it is not second taste permitting the first, as explained in siman 95; and in truth Beit Yosef wrote: the reason is there is no power in taste this excrement received, etc. — fish that received meat taste are permitted to join afterward with milk, and all the more here since it is excrement and a nullified matter with no power to receive meat taste and carry it to join with cheese milk to make basar b'chalav — worse than second taste since pure excrement — end; and he understood here too it is second taste permitting the first, only because excrement it is worse than second taste permitting the first — but it is not so, for here it is not second taste permitting the first at all, as I wrote — rather since pure excrement it is worse than second taste permitting the first — and this is clear — end."]`,
  '10#ז': `["And in a case of loss one may be lenient. And even per what is written in s.k. 25 that even when actually dried some are stringent — it appears here in clear and congealed one may also be lenient in great loss, since there is only a rabbinic prohibition, and one may rely in such a case on responsum of R' Shimon bar Avraham in Mordechai and responsum of Maimonides, and so is the view of Semak and ShaDa; and further that in the sage general rule 79 law 3 he wrote b'dieved one may rely on those who are lenient — it appears even without great loss; if so he equates the law of clear and congealed entirely to dried law; and if so, even per those who say where there is no loss one should be stringent even when dried — here too in clear and congealed; and further, Rif, Rambam, other poskim, and Mechaber among them permit even when clear; Bach in siman 81 ruled regarding actually dried like the Rav, and regarding clear and congealed wrote we practice to be stringent on a d'oraisa prohibition and it has the law of clear, even in great loss — not like hagahah of Shulchan Aruch siman 87 — end; and it is astonishing: what d'oraisa prohibition is here? From the Torah it is forbidden only by way of cooking, and this milk in the stomach was forbidden only by salting or pickling; perhaps he refers only to milk found in a kosher stomach that nursed from a tereifah — but his wording implies all cases; and further, if so what did he challenge against the Rav? Nevertheless it appears one may permit even in kosher stomach that nursed from tereifah when clear and congealed in great loss, for many poskim permit even when clear; if so all those poskim are fit from the outset to equate clear and congealed to dried, to rely on them in such a case; and one may also say Rashba who wrote in Torat HaBayit the short version 'congealed in the animal's lifetime' — his words are not a decisive ruling; one may say he stated the usual way to equate with all those poskim above; and in Torat HaBayit the long version he did not mention this at all; on the contrary it appears the opposite, for he wrote in Torat HaBayit the long version (section 1 side 2): and Rabbenu Tam's words appear that what we say one may set rennet in milk in the stomach means when dried, for all that congealed has the law of excrement; and the usual way is one does not set rennet in clear milk except in dried — end; it appears he came to explain what the tanna teaches without distinguishing dried and clear — because the usual way is one sets in dried; and if you say clear and congealed he holds forbidden — still the tanna need not teach anonymously; rather certainly once congealed afterward it is considered congealed in the animal's lifetime; also what Beit Yosef wrote that it appears from all poskim we require congealed in the animal's lifetime is not required at all — end."]`,
  '10#ח': `["Stomach skin sometimes, etc. And the same applies to other innards when they dry them until they become like wood; nevertheless it appears l'chatchila one should not do so — end."]`,
  '11#א': `["If there is meat taste. Meaning: when there is not sixty in the cheese against the skin; and Mechaber follows his view that below siman 98 we rely on gentile taste — therefore he wrote anonymously 'if there is meat taste'; but we do not rely nowadays on gentile taste, as the Rav wrote in hagahah there — therefore we always measure with sixty; and this is what the Rav wrote in hagahah — the wording is 'if there are sixty' — investigate — end."]`,
  '11#ב': `["But one who sets rennet in nevelah stomach skin. The distinction in this poskim wrote: kosher stomach skin, since permitted in itself and has no prohibition except because of its joining with cheese — whenever it does not impart taste it is not basar b'chalav but each stands alone; but nevelah skin and the like, forbidden in itself — when it sets we see the prohibition as if in existence — end; and Maharshal ruled in chapter 25 siman 106 that even one who sets in nevelah stomach skin nullifies in sixty; and his words are not required — end."]`,
  '11#ג': `["But if there was also permitted setting agent, etc. It appears specifically when the prohibition cannot set by itself without the permitted — if not so, forbidden; and requires study for practical law; and see below siman 143 seif 4 and seif 11 and what is written there — end."]`,
};

export const TAZ_087 = {
  '1#_': `That it is not forbidden from the Torah is permitted for benefit. Maharshal chapter 25 siman 100 disagrees with this and with what Rambam and Mordechai wrote; he holds it is reasonable to be stringent for benefit. And we have only the words of Rishonim and Acharonim.`,
  '3#א': `Permitted in cooking and benefit. Tur wrote: but pure meat in impure milk or the reverse, and wild animal meat, etc. — is only rabbinically forbidden and permitted in cooking, etc. Beit Yosef challenged: if one species is impure it is forbidden in eating d'oraisa — why decree basar b'chalav eating prohibition? Derishah and Orach Chaim resolve there is practical difference regarding piece becoming nevelah — R' Ephraim siman 92 holds only with meat and milk; and another difference. Acharonim wondered why Sages decreed since already forbidden for impure meat — where hinted? Possibly tanna teaches pure domestic meat in impure milk permitted in cooking and benefit — that 'permitted in cooking and benefit' is superfluous; should say merely permitted — clear it refers to eating; when eating impure thing we need precision that eating forbidden because of basar b'chalav. In any case clear from Mishnah: impure domestic animal permitted cooking and benefit; Rashi did not mention eating permitted since impurity prohibition exists — learn without impurity no basar b'chalav connection; if not tanna would mention; rather plain impure species like other prohibitions; clear Tur's 'only rabbinically' refers only to fowl, not impure meat/milk together — only regarding permitted in benefit and cooking as Beit Yosef; Mechaber well did not copy this; Orach Chaim wrote incorrect; it appears to me law is clear; per Maharshal's correction in Tur before 'and wild animal' — Tur intends only to teach leniency in cooking and benefit.`,
  '3#ב': `But fish, etc. Beit Yosef wrote nevertheless do not eat fish in milk because of danger — error; should be with meat as Orach Chaim siman 173; Darkei Moshe confused Beit Yosef here; Levush copied Beit Yosef incorrectly.`,
  '3#ג': `As explained above siman 66 regarding blood. Darkei Moshe and sage general rule 62: Maharshal learned from fish blood to forbid fowl in almond milk without almonds for marit ayin; rejected — no proof from blood which has karet unlike rabbinic fowl in milk; even domestic animal forbidden only by cooking — no marit ayin concern; Rama's words correct and custom spread; nevertheless l'chatchila place almonds by fowl too; where no almonds do not forbid eating — all we can fix we fix; no worse than woman's milk where marit ayin concerned; where no almonds do not forbid eating like woman's milk nearby; Rama's wording implies b'dieved or impossible to place does not block eating.`,
  '4#_': `And it appears per this, etc. Darkei Moshe left Rashba's words on woman's milk in wonder — not a fortiori from impure milk permitted in cooking; his view here is very astonishing; in Torat HaBayit Rashba explicitly permitted impure milk in cooking and benefit without marit ayin; it appears they did not decree marit ayin except where each alone is permitted to eat — then resembles pure meat and milk; therefore with almond milk l'chatchila place almonds; woman's milk permitted when separated as siman 81 — forbidden to cook with meat for marit ayin; unlike impure milk or meat — no resemblance to pure basar b'chalav — no decree there; clear per my opinion; though I cannot lenient regarding what Rama stringent; nevertheless plain one must forbid marit ayin cooking fowl in milk — Sages decreed explicitly; for one tanna truly d'oraisa; Darkei Moshe: fowl in milk worse than impure in milk — not as Levush who permits marit ayin because recognizable — sometimes cut unrecognizable; viewer cannot distinguish fowl from domestic meat; actually dispute of tannaim; Maharshal chapter 25 siman 52: more concern lest say fowl in milk permitted like R' Yosi HaGelili; Rama's l'chatchila here and nearby regarding dead milk for cooking — be careful where no great need; for medical need without danger may permit cooking in milk where eating prohibition only rabbinic — all the more impure milk or meat.`,
  '5#_': `And permitted to eat it in milk. Maharshal challenged: why unlike Rashba/Tur siman 86 striking chicken on tail — if entangled in sinews like meat from living; answered: different there d'oraisa eiver min ha'chai prohibition; here Mechaber wrote not completed — even with yolk and white fit for milk even entangled; reason: no meat taste at all; when struck while entangled considered meat from living — not taste-dependent; Maharshal: strained for law — whenever entangled considered meat for all matters; Mahari Weil: women practice prohibition even hard shell; l'chatchila stringent; b'dieved when shell complete even white membrane suffices — no meat law; see siman 87 regarding salting these eggs in name of Maharshal.`,
  '9#_': `And some forbid. Meaning clear only; Rabbenu Tam and Rashi forbid even dried — not mere excrement; Rama ruled like Rabbenu Tam; Maharshal chapter 25 siman 107 like Rashi: great leniency to permit dried — who knows measure of drying; possibly only if dried with no moisture at all; primary like Rashi.`,
  '10#א': `That it was salted in stomach. Measure to stand in salting to count as boiling — siman 69 seif 18; salting measure as boiling — siman 91 seif 5.`,
  '10#ב': `Forbidden to set rennet in it. Meaning do not use that salted/stood-one-day milk to curdle other milk for cheese.`,
  '10#ג': `Until sixty in milk. Difficult per Rosh siman 108: min b'mino in sixty as Beit Yosef; here salting context — Rosh holds salting forbids only peel measure; cooking needs sixty; say since milk here like oil prohibition forbids whole piece per siman 105; though end siman 105 Rashba etc. — Rosh and Tur disagree; siman 105 seif 7 implies milk not like oil prohibition — investigate; resolve: pickled forbidden entirely per Rosh; salting in vessel measure siman 69; Beit Yosef here proves; Tur/Beit Yosef only full day; Rav hagahah follows his view all salting in sixty as siman 105.`,
  '10#ד': `And if stomach was dried it does not forbid. Mere excrement; nevertheless forbids meat taste absorbed in excrement — Beit Yosef: second taste permitting first like fish in bowl; Darkei Moshe disagrees — not second taste; absorbed taste in milk is first taste.`,
  '11#א': `One who set rennet in skin, etc. Meaning placed piece of skin in milk.`,
  '11#ב': `This-and-this causes. Learned from Mordechai: Rabbenu baruch permitted — presumably did not set all pot milk from forbidden stomach only from dried; not customary to put stomach in pot; sometimes more dried — this-and-this causes; permitted; if prohibition cannot leaven alone without permitted — all permit; if prohibition alone can leaven — not this-and-this causes; Mordechai: if prohibition alone cannot leaven without help — permitted; Abaye Avodah Zarah 49 — this-and-this causes only if neither alone can leaven; if prohibition alone can — help does not help; Tosafot there explicitly; here clear dried cannot set alone — cannot rely on this-and-this causes without proof no clear in stomach; Mordechai's words require study.`,
};

